That's where the "exponentially nastier" bit comes in. As the
superpuds get larger, they gradually get more dangerous: Losing
respect for E, getting higher levels of magic (ie taming[1])
resistance, quicker HP regeneration, more speed, bigger and nastier
bite attacks, shock attacks, digestion attacks, more attacks per turn
and whatever else seems appropriately evil. Even the lowly brown
pudding could start to get proper attacks as it gained in size.

Hmmm. Perhaps a neater way to do this would be to add a "mutation"
factor to every pudding split [2]: When you spilt a pudding, there's a
chance the offspring will acquire one of the abilities above. Then
have merged puddings adopt not only the combined HP and MAXHP of both
component puds[3], but any and all special abilities. You can see how
a true superpud could be inadvertently created by an overambitious
farmer. What's more, it's rate of growth and the order in which its
special abilities emerged would be far less predictable than through
some linear "If pudding size=X add special ability Y" formula.

For this to really work and have the potential to take on momentum and
spiral beyond the player's control, you'd want to implement not only
spontaneous re-merging of puds, but also occasional spontaneous
splitting: Not enough that a superpud is likely to appear all on its
own, but enough that a level with more than a dozen puds on it (a
situation only likely to occur as a result of deliberate farming)
could quickly take on momentum and get beyond the player's control.

Of course the rates and probablilities of splitting/remerging/mutation
would have to be established by a lot of trial-and-error playtesting
to get the balance right. You want it to be slow at first, so that a
non-farmer or very careful, small scale organic pudding farmer is
unlikely to unleash a blob of doom, but that any attempts at large-
scale factory farming will quickly snowball into mayhem. This means
merges and spontaneous splits have to be fairly rare in a small
population, but increasingly common with larger populations.

For this reason it may be advantageous to add two more possible
mutations: "often merges" and "often splits spontaneously". Once one
of these traits appears in a population, things will really start to
get interesting. The fact that puddings can merge and split and re-
merge, sharing their abilities with every interaction, means that any
mutation left unchecked will eventually infect the entire population.
In this light, a population of puds can almost be viewed as one
organism.[4]
Therefore, once you get "often merges" and "often splits" in the same
population, that's when you know you're in trouble. Each pudding
effectively becomes a self-feeding pudding factory, spitting out puds
(which spit out more puds) and then re-absorbing them to become even
stronger, increasing in size and mutations exponentially. It would be
very hard to kill, not least because of the legions of offspring
puddings that you have to wade through to get to it. I suppose you
could keep a genocide handy for such situations, but that would put
paid to farming for that game, wouldn't it?

"Often merges", on its own, would of course ultimately result in one
giant superpud that rarely splits. Play-testing will of course be the
proof of the pudding (narf), but I suspect that for a real challenge
you'd want "often splits" to appear far more often than, or usually in
conjunction with "often merges". Maybe the challenge will be enough
(after all, it doesn't have to be a complete nightmare *every* time a
mutation appears) but if not, just rebalance the two so that one is
more probable or combine them into a single ability.

More evil possible mutation effects:

