I know exactly what you mean, which is why I prefer the "bigger uglier
pudding" approach.

The pudding debate has been simmering away on rgrn for years now I can
sympathise with both sides of it. Therefore I like the idea of a
solution that fixes it without removing it from the game entirely.
Finding a risk that is worthy of the massive rewards of farming is
difficult, but I think the only way to do it is to somehow link it to
the quantities of puddings on a level: If there are only a few, then
they are the mostly-harmless dessert monsters we know and love. When
they appear in huge hordes, they become more and more dangerous.
Rather than a heavy-handed outright ban it or nerfing of farming, I
think farming should become its own challenge/deterrent/punishment
(for those not sufficiently challenged/ deterred/ punished by boredom.
[5]) To me, there's a nethackish elegance to that.


[1] A second counter to taming would be to allow tame puds to merge
with hostile ones, with a very strong chance of the result being
hostile.
[2] Anyone starting a debate here on the genetics of pudding splits
and the chance of mutation really needs to get out more.
[3] Actually, I would have a pud merger add the HP and MAXHP of the
two puds, and then add a small random number (say 1D4-1) to both stats
as well: The merged pudding should be greater than the sum of its
parts.
[4] It would actually be quite fascinating to study a runaway pud-
population (from a safe distance, of course). If I could code I would
happily write this patch just as an "artificial life" type toy.
[5] Yes, I know, there's a bot to do away with the boredom, but that
actually re-inforces my point.


